Z||000002||KRMFiero||06-21-2001||10:07 AM||||Maybe its time to look for a 2.8 to replace it? the 87's were the good duke's a complete rebuild kit is only like 200$ - you could get it bored like .20 over and even make some more power out of it...  I wouldnt keep buying more Fieros i would fix it but im not you so...||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||06-21-2001||12:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaveL:<BR><B>...the constant ticking in my 87 2.5 SE turned suddenly into a loud clatter. I heard a muffled sort of pow, then it coughed and died. Big cloud of white smoke from the exhaust, and it won't even turn over...you can't really tell what happened till you open up the engine...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My guess?  One exhaust valve decided to quit operating...  might be able to find out by pulling off the rocker cover.  Just a guess, but might be worth trying...  ||||reg||3
Z||000004||theogre||06-21-2001||07:59 PM||||Don't even waste time on it....<P>head to the nearest scrap yard and find another one in a Grand Am or Olds Cutlass Calais. these things are all over the place and cheap. ($200-300, maybe less) You can install another motor in a day or two. (2-3 if you've never done one before.)<P>The engines are identical (91 olds is rated for more HP but I suspect that the Fiero one is actually underated by GM. [There are several reasons you would under rate an engine... It's actually a fairly common practice.])<P>You will need to change pulleys and brackets but other wise they are the same. do youself a favor and just get a new tensioner for the belt. The one you have is probably pretty old.<P>I'm planning over the summer to find a good one and crate it for future use.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
